1 NOTE Superintendent Gus Battles was born January 28, 1905 in Tyler, Smith County, Tex
2 CONC as to the parentage of Mr. and Mrs. Horatio Battles.     Gus lived with his pare
2 CONC nts until the age of thirteen when he left Tyler and moved to Fort Worth, Texas.
2 CONC       Gus and Miss Angie Wallace were united in marriage, July 25, 1922. To this
2 CONC  union five children were born, two of which preceded Gus in death.      Gus con
2 CONC fessed to Christ and received the Baptism of the Holy Ghost under the leadership
2 CONC  of Elder C. Olga Love in 1923, where Gus served faithfully as a member. He beca
2 CONC me a licensed and ordained minister under the leadership of Bishop E. E. Hamilto
2 CONC n and served as his assistant pastor for five years and assistant pastor under t
2 CONC he leadership of Bishop S.D. Lee at the Love Chapel C.O.G.I.C. for seven years u
2 CONC ntil he heard the call to go into another vineyard to watch over the flocks of t
2 CONC he master.    Gus’s first pastorate was at the Vickery Blvd. Church of God in Ch
2 CONC rist, where he served faithfully and God blessed his work. He felt the urgency t
2 CONC o build God a house, and they moved to 1020 Bryan Street on the Southside in For
2 CONC t Worth, Texas. The church was named Love Sanctuary C.O.G.I.C., and Gus served a
2 CONC s a faithful and loyal servant until his home going.  He was also called to Grap
2 CONC evine, Texas to pastor, and for fifteen years, he served as shepherd of the floc
2 CONC k and built a church and left it free of indebtedness.     During his pastorate,
2 CONC  Gus held the following positions: President of the Fort Worth District Y. P. W.
2 CONC  W., Treasurer of the State Youth Department of Texas N. E., and Superintendent 
2 CONC of the Stop-Six District.      Gus was one of the first three Negroes to be a li
2 CONC censed and bonded as a contractor by the City of Fort Worth.     Battles and Son
2 CONC s Cement Contractors was established in 1947.
