1 NOTE According to the 1900 Census Report, Horatio Battles was born January 1870, but 
2 CONC Myrtle Hunt Thomas has his birthday as September 29, 1865. Myrtle remembers Hora
2 CONC tio being 79 years old when he died in the summer of 1944. <br><br>Horatio marri
2 CONC ed Georgia Ann Jones on 	January 8, 1885 in Smith County. To this union, two chi
2 CONC ldren were born: Anthony Battles and Easter Battles. Anthony served in the Unite
2 CONC d States Army and spent most of his time in France. His wife’s name was Bertha. 
2 CONC They had no children. Bertha wrote a letter to Settie Hunt in December 1964 from
2 CONC  Watonga, Oklahoma after placing Anthony in a rest home in Geary, Oklahoma. Anth
2 CONC ony was living in Oklahoma when he died. Easter died as a young adult.<br><br>Ho
2 CONC ratio married Elizabeth “Lizzie” Carey on 	March 1, 1896. Whoever filled out the
2 CONC  license misspelled Lizzie’s last name as “Carry.”<br><br>Horatio and Elizabeth’
2 CONC s Children:<br><br>1.	Edmond Battles; born March 8, 1896, Tyler, Texas; died Aug
2 CONC ust 1957, Tyler, Texas. Buried at Oliver Chapel Cemetery.<br>2.	Settie Alma Batt
2 CONC les; born September 5, 1898, Tyler, Texas; died December 1991, Fort Worth, Texas
2 CONC . Buried at Skyvue Memorial Gardens, Mansfield, Texas.<br>3.	Sam Calvin Battles;
2 CONC  born March 12, 1900, Tyler, Texas; died November 27, 1972, Fort Worth, Texas. B
2 CONC uried at Cedar Hill Memorial Park.<br>4.	Johnnie Mae Battles; born January 5, 19
2 CONC 03, Tyler, Texas; died March 25, 1974, Tyler, Texas.<br>5.	Augustus “Gus” Battle
2 CONC s; born January 28, 1905, Tyler, Texas; died February 22, 1965, Fort Worth, Texa
2 CONC s. Buried at Skyvue Memorial Gardens, Rendon, Texas.<br>6.	Clemie Battles; born 
2 CONC 1906; died 1920.<br>7.	Luther “Chap” Battles; born 1908, Tyler, Texas; died Octo
2 CONC ber 19, 1930.<br>8.	James Thomas “J.T.” Battles; born June 25, 1911, Tyler, Texa
2 CONC s; died July 30, 1970, Tyler, Texas. Buried at Oliver Chapel Cemetery.<br>9.	L.J
2 CONC . Battles; born March 9, 1915, Tyler, Texas; died February 27, 1984, Fort Worth,
2 CONC  Texas. Buried at Cedar Hill Memorial Park.<br>10.	Nathaniel Battles, born 1918,
2 CONC  Tyler, Texas; died October 1952. Buried at Oliver Chapel Cemetery.<br><br>Accor
2 CONC ding to family members, Horatio and Elizabeth had two additional daughters who e
2 CONC ach died at very young ages. All of Horatio and Elizabeth’s children were born w
2 CONC hile Horatio and Elizabeth lived in a log house on Judge Barnes’ Place.<br><br>A
2 CONC  Mason and a deacon with good hair when he was a young man, Horatio was known as
2 CONC  “Rashe”. His nickname was often misspelled. On their applications for a Social 
2 CONC Security Number, Gus spelled his father’s name “Rash,” L.J. spelled it “Reich,” 
2 CONC and Sam spelled it “Rache.” <br><br>Horatio farmed, but he also possessed an exc
2 CONC eptional talent for working with wood. He could rive boards to provide a lifetim
2 CONC e roof for log houses. White people came from near and far to have Horatio build
2 CONC  log houses. Horatio, his oldest son Edmond, and Horatio’s half-brother, John Jo
2 CONC hnson, would <br>cut, shape, and notch logs with mallets and axes and construct 
2 CONC log houses using a mix of sage grass and white clay. If Horatio built or roofed 
2 CONC a log house, his customers knew his work would be high quality and that his roof
2 CONC s and log houses would not leak. <br><br>Horatio’s index finger on his right han
2 CONC d was crooked from being broken by riving logs. Horatio also made walking canes 
2 CONC and wooden knitting needles. He always kept half of a cow and a hog in a smoke h
2 CONC ouse. He had a special rub that could keep the meat from spoiling for almost a y
2 CONC ear. <br><br>Lizzie harvested Irish and sweet potatoes from her garden every yea
2 CONC r. Shortly after Lizzie died in 1934, a fire destroyed Horatio’s house on Judge 
2 CONC Barnes’ place. Most of the family’s photographs were lost in the fire. Horatio s
2 CONC uspected the fire was intentionally set. He had an idea who set it, but no one w
2 CONC as ever identified as being responsible. <br><br>After Elizabeth died in 1934, H
2 CONC oratio married a lady who has been referred to only as Mrs. Holden.<br><br>At th
2 CONC e time of his death in 1944, Horatio was living with his sons, Edmond, Nathaniel
2 CONC , and Nathaniel’s wife Carrie in Jack Yancey’s place on Ark Road. Johnnie Battle
2 CONC s, who married L.J. Battles in 1945, remembers seeing Horatio in Fort Worth on a
2 CONC  single occasion. Horatio was in a car. Although Horatio and Lizzie are buried a
2 CONC t the Oliver Chapel Cemetery, they are not buried next to each other. Their grav
2 CONC esites are not marked.
