1 NOTE Elbert Lee Domino Sr.: A Legacy of Dedication and Faith<br><br>In the quaint tow
2 CONC n of Overton, Texas, on May 7, 1908, Elbert Lee Domino Sr. first opened his eyes
2 CONC  to the world. Born to the loving embrace of Jimmy and Lula Ford Domino, Elbert 
2 CONC was the fifth of six siblings who grew up in the heart of a farming community, i
2 CONC nstilling in him values of hard work, resilience, and dedication.<br><br>In a co
2 CONC mmunity where everyone knew each other, Elbert carved a niche for himself by own
2 CONC ing and operating a café. More than a place to grab a meal, this café played a p
2 CONC ivotal role in his life—it was the place where his heart found its counterpart i
2 CONC n Pearlene Battles. Serving customers and sharing smiles, their camaraderie flou
2 CONC rished into love. And on September 29, 1950, they decided to embrace life's jour
2 CONC ney together as a married couple. Together, they built a family of seven childre
2 CONC n, who not only were testament to their love but also to their shared values.<br
2 CONC ><br>Elbert's childhood on a farm had ignited a lifelong passion for farming. Wh
2 CONC ile some men of his age would be looking towards retirement, Elbert was often se
2 CONC en at the Dallas Farmers Market, beaming with pride as he showcased his produce.
2 CONC  His unyielding work ethic was not only evident in his business but also in the 
2 CONC principles he passed on to his children.<br><br>More than his entrepreneurial en
2 CONC deavors, it was Elbert's benevolence that endeared him to many. In his eyes, eve
2 CONC ry person was a friend, and his hands were always extended to help those in need
2 CONC . His unwavering faith in Jesus was the backbone of his actions. He lived the te
2 CONC achings he believed in, ensuring that his children imbibed them not just through
2 CONC  his words but through his daily actions.<br><br>His commitment to his faith was
2 CONC  evident in his affiliation with the Sharon Seventh Day Adventist in Tyler, Texa
2 CONC s. As someone who practiced what he preached, Elbert earned not just the respect
2 CONC  but also the admiration of the community.<br><br>Elbert Lee Domino Sr., a symbo
2 CONC l of steadfastness and love, breathed his last on March 2, 2000. His legacy, how
2 CONC ever, remains imprinted on the sands of time, in the hearts of his family, and i
2 CONC n the memories of the community. A beacon of faith, love, and dedication, Elbert
2 CONC ’s life serves as a testimony to the power of leading by example.
