1 NOTE Mrs. Angie Wallace (Baby) Battles, born April 6, the youngest of 12 children to 
2 CONC Mr. and Mrs. Lewis  Wallace Sr. in Marion County, Jefferson, Texas, succumbed to
2 CONC  a lengthy illness, Thursday, December 4,  1980 at a local convalescent center. 
2 CONC  She became a resident of Fort Worth at an early age. She met the late Superinte
2 CONC ndent Gus Battles and they  were married on July 25, 1922. To this union five ch
2 CONC ildren were born. Two of the children and a fosterdaughter  yet live.  Mother Ba
2 CONC ttles became a Christian at an early age and she began her church work then. She
2 CONC  was saved at  the age of 16 and received the Baptism of the Holy Ghost. She wor
2 CONC ked in the church faithfully, serving as  church secretary, Sunshine Band presid
2 CONC ent, Home and Foreign Mission president, Usher Board President,  and Mother`s Bo
2 CONC ard president. Her only church home was "White Street" Church of God In Christ, 
2 CONC later  named "East Rosedale" COGIC, and still later "Love Chapel" COGIC. Her pas
2 CONC tors were: the late Elder E.  E. Hamilton, and Elder S. D. Lee. When her husband
2 CONC , the late Superintendent G. Battles began pastoring  Love Sanctuary, she began 
2 CONC her role as Pastor`s wife. They labored at the West Vickery Street COGIC,  which
2 CONC  later moved to 1020 Bryan and the church was renamed "Love Sanctuary". They bui
2 CONC lt the edifice on  Bryan St. and they also labored for over 15 years at Grapevin
2 CONC e, Texas where they erected the present  Sanctuary.  She is known throughout the
2 CONC  city and state for her many charitable deeds. She is widely known for her  spir
2 CONC it of hospitality, She served as president of the Minister`s Wives of the city a
2 CONC nd district. Under the  supervision of the late Mother Bertha M. Polk, she worke
2 CONC d with all of the auxiliaries and received the  appointment of president of the 
2 CONC City-wide Women`s auxiliary union of Fort Worth and District Missionary  of the 
2 CONC Denton district. Other areas served included president of the "Ever-Ready" Circl
2 CONC e, Co-state president  of hospitality, and Usher Board counselor for the volunte
2 CONC er mission.  Mother Battles became the "Church Mother" in 1965 through the recom
2 CONC mendation and appointment of her  son Elder E. L. Battles, who took up the mantl
2 CONC e from his father`s pastoral work. She remained in that office  until the Lord c
2 CONC alled her home.  In 1978 at the Sheraton Hotel, Mother Battles was named "Mother
2 CONC  of the Year" by the F. Brooks and Grays  Literary and Art Club for her distingu
2 CONC ished service in the community. Mother Battles was a succourer of  many and her 
2 CONC daily motto "Lord help me to live from day-to-day in such a self forget forgetfu
2 CONC l way, that  even when I kneel to pray, my prayer shall be for others." She is s
2 CONC urvived by her beloved son, Dr. E. L.  Battles, her daughter, Mrs. Dorothy R. Le
2 CONC nzy, her foster daughter, Mrs. Mary Stewart, 15 grandchildren, 11  great grandch
2 CONC ildren, nieces, nephews, other relatives and a host of friends  Early in her mar
2 CONC riage to Gus, Angie worked as a maid for some affluent  white people in Fort Wor
2 CONC th. She is credited for getting Gus introduced to  men who helped him finance Ba
2 CONC ttles & Sons Cement Contractors.  Baby was an excellent cook, and she taught her
2 CONC  children be good cooks also.  Known for her homemade ginger bread, pound cake, 
2 CONC and sweet potato pies,  Baby’s specialty was homemade rolls. She passed her reci
2 CONC pe down to her  granddaughter, Angela Eileen Lenzy.
